<strong>The short answer:</strong> the most practical way to <strong><a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/how-can-interior-design-graduates-get-work-experience/">gain interior design work experience</a></strong> as a fresher is to get involved in real interior design projects through an internship, where you can learn how a professional studio works, contribute to genuine design tasks and build experience that goes beyond a university portfolio.

Finishing an interior design course can feel like reaching the starting line rather than the finish line. You may understand design principles, know your way around software and have several academic projects to show, but many entry-level roles still ask for practical experience. For actionable guidance, read our overview on <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/how-to-get-an-interior-design-internship-portfolio-skills-and-london-opportunities/">how to get an interior design internship in London</a>.

So, how do you bridge that gap?
<h2>Why Is Work Experience Important for Fresh Interior Designers?</h2>
A portfolio can demonstrate that you can design a room. Work experience demonstrates that you understand what happens afterwards.

Professional projects involve much more than choosing colours and furniture. You may encounter client revisions, supplier communication, material sourcing, technical drawings, budgets, deadlines and decisions that need to be made quickly.

An internship gives fresh designers the opportunity to see these processes first-hand, learning how an <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/interior-designers-london/">award-winning London interior design team</a> coordinates with clients, contractors, and craftsmen.

It also helps you understand something university projects cannot fully replicate: <strong>designing for a real person, a real property and real constraints.</strong>
<h2>What Can You Learn During an Interior Design Internship?</h2>
