Not every internship provides the same learning experience.

The value comes from being involved.

At Oraanj Interiors, our approach is to give aspiring designers exposure to the practical side of interior design. You can learn more <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/about/">about Oraanj Interior Design studio</a> and how our practice bridges design creativity with real-world project delivery. Rather than treating an intern as someone who simply observes from the sidelines, an internship can provide opportunities to understand how a professional design studio approaches projects.

This might involve seeing how concepts develop, how materials are selected, how drawings support a project and how designers communicate their ideas.

The experience helps connect the theory learned during education with the reality of professional practice, especially when dealing with complex residential briefs like our <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/barbican-residence-interior-design/">Barbican modernist apartment case study</a>.
<h3>Do You Need Experience Before Applying for an Internship?</h3>
No, and that is one of the main reasons internships exist.

You do not need to arrive knowing everything.

What matters is curiosity, willingness to learn and the ability to take feedback. Basic knowledge of relevant design software and a portfolio of your strongest work can help, but your portfolio does not need to look like it was produced by someone with ten years of experience.

A fresher's portfolio should show potential.

Show how you think, not just how polished your final images look. Understanding diverse client demographics is also key—such as how studios cater to overseas clients in our guide on <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/london-property-interior-design-for-expats-and-international-investors-what-you-need-to-know/">London property interior design for expats and international investors</a>.
<h3>What Happens After the Internship?</h3>
<img class="alignnone wp-image-8845 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Interior-Design-Internships-in-London-1.png" alt="" width="1920" height="1111" />

An internship doesn't guarantee a permanent job, and it shouldn't be presented that way.

What it can provide is momentum.

You leave with a better understanding of professional design, stronger examples of your experience and a clearer idea of which areas of the industry interest you.

For some designers, that leads to junior roles—an ambition we explore in depth on how to <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/turn-interior-design-internship-into-full-time-job/">turn an interior design internship into a full time job</a>. For others, it helps them improve their portfolio before applying elsewhere or decide that a particular area of interior design isn't right for them.

Either way, the experience has value.
<h4>Your First Step Into the Industry Doesn't Have to Be Perfect</h4>
Freshers often put too much pressure on themselves to find the perfect first job.

Instead, look for an opportunity where you can learn.

